  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThru Friday...Strong surface high pressure over the Appalachians\nwill combine with lower pressures to the south across the Gulf/FL\nStraits and eventually southwest Atlc to keep a tight pressure \ngradient over the area. East winds 15-25 mph each day with gusts \nup to 35 mph. Occasional gusts to 40 mph are possible at times \nalong the coast. Winds will remain breezy/gusty along the coast\nduring the nighttime, and holding at 10-15 mph over the interior.\n\nMoisture increases ahead of weakening low pressure over the Gulf \ncombined with a disturbance aloft to produce ISOLD-SCT (20-50%) to\nlocally numerous (60%) PoPs today. Coverage increases further \ntonight (60-70%) and into Wed-Thu (60-80%) as low-topped showery \nprecip develops across the local coastal waters and at the coast \npushing onshore. A Flood Watch goes into effect at 5 pm and no\nchanges have been made to its spatial or temporal scale. Continue\nto keep Lake county out of the Flood watch given less soil \nsaturation and better ability to absorb several inches over a few \ndays. Primary impacts with showers and ISOLD lightning storms will\nbe occasional lightning strikes, gusty winds locally 30-45 mph \nand torrential downpours. Widespread rainfall amounts of 4-6\" are \nforecast with locally 8-12\" closer to the coast. There is a small \nchance for highly localized amounts of 12\"+ focused primarily over\nportions of Volusia and/or Brevard counties. Do not drive through\nflooded roadways if you encounter flooding! \n\nAfter collaborating with WPC, a Moderate risk (level 3 out of \n4) of excessive rainfall exists for the next three days (Wed-Fri)\nalong portions of the NE and EC FL Atlc coast with Slight risk\n(Level 2 out of 4) for much of the interior. It is unusual for WPC\nto issue a Moderate risk at Day 4. \n\nA High Surf Advisory goes into effect this evening as winds and \nseas build over the local coastal waters. Rough, pounding surf \nwill promote moderate to locally major beach erosion along the \ncoast. In addition, a HIGH rip current threat will exist. Entering\nthe rough and dangerous surf is strongly discouraged!\n\nSat-Mon...Models are in fairly good agreement of developing low \npressure near the northern Bahamas Fri and lifting northward \noffshore the Florida east coast Sat. ERLY winds will gradually \ndecrease into the weekend with direction becoming north/northeast\nSat afternoon and further \"backing\" to the north/northwest Sat \nnight-Mon around the counter-clockwise circulation of the low \npressure. This should pull in some drier air over the area Sun- \nMon.",
